WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:05.880
Hey Wisecrack! Jared here.

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:08.120
And today, we’re exploring the bizarre antics of

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:10.940
movie star / performance artist / internet meme

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:12.060
Shia LaBeouf.

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:12.660
Shia: "DO IT!"

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:13.340
"DO IT!"

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:14.160
"DO IT!"

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:15.020
"JUST DO IT!"

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:15.880
"DO IT!"

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:17.220
"DO IT!  "DdoO IitT!!!" "DO IT"
"Do It!"  "do it!"

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:21.220
While Shia is perhaps best known for his breakout
role in Evens Stevens

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:23.220
and his later commercial successes

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:27.480
it's been his smaller projects and erratic
off-screen antics that have transformed him

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:30.380
from a movie star into a kind of meme artist.

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:31.780
"When the seagulls..."

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:34.260
*gulp*

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:36.480
"...follow the trawler..."

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:37.740
*wipe*

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:41.680
...it is because they think 
sardines will be thrown into the sea."

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:43.420
Thank you very much

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:46.280
Growing up is hard, especially for child actors

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:48.000
and Shia is no exception.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.520
His life of fame seems to have left him with
very few boundaries

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:54.100
and an almost masochistic love of self-mockery.

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:56.820
When musician Rob Cantor wanted to shoot a
music video

00:00:56.820 --> 00:01:00.060
for his song about a rampaging cannibal 
known as Shia-LaBeouf

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:01.160
♫ Shia LaBeouf! ♫

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:03.200
Shia was not only willing to participate

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:06.600
but, suggested that he reenact the clapping scene from Citizen Kane.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:09.880
Why does Shia have his own entry on the knowyourmeme.com?

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:12.040
Is he just another actor like William Shatner

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:15.500
or Chuck Norris who is willing to 
cash in on his fame through self-parody

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:17.420
or, is something
else going on here?

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:19.520
Shia: "I went through, like, an existential crisis

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:20.040
Ellen: "Uh-huh"

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:21.620
Shia: "Which turned into some kind of like uh..."

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:23.140
"...e-exploration, really."

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:25.640
Welcome to  this Wisecrack edition 
on the Philosophy of

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:26.720
Shia LaBeouf.

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:30.760
For nearly a decade Shia Labeouf has bounced
from one tabloid scandal to another.

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:35.140
Back in 2007, he was arrested in a Chicago
Walgreens on his 21st birthday.

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:36.640
Shia: "I got pretty wasted in Chicago"

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:38.280
"and wound up celebrating in Walgreens."

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:41.820
In 2011, he was recorded getting in a 
fight outside a bar in Vancouver

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:43.040
but wasn't arrested.

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:45.920
And in 2014 , after “drinking a lot of whiskey,”

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:47.700
Shia: "So, I had been drinking a lot of whiskey."

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:49.700
LaBeouf interrupted a performance of Cabaret

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:53.070
by accosting Alan Cumming as he was making
his way to the stage.

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:54.370
Alan: "He was just wasted."

00:01:54.370 --> 00:01:55.870
"And he, like, whacked me."

00:01:55.870 --> 00:01:56.930
"He did say that the reason he did it"

00:01:56.930 --> 00:01:58.930
"was that I was the 'sexiest man' he had ever seen, so

00:01:58.930 --> 00:01:59.810
*cautious audience laughter*

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:04.030
But his most significant controversy occurred
when he plagiarized Daniel Clowes comic

00:02:04.030 --> 00:02:05.400
"Justin M Damiano"

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:08.020
using it as the basis for his 2012 short film

00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:09.560
Howard Cantour.com.

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:12.000
After HowardCantour.com premiered at Cannes

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.560
it was released online where fans of Clowes’ comic

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:16.960
inevitably found it and tore it apart.

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:20.000
Exposed, Labeouf needed an excuse to explain
himself.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:24.580
First, he tried to explain the plagiarism
as an act of “postmodern” re-appropriation

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:28.280
claiming that he was undermining the idea
of originality and authorship

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:30.240
Shia: "I was looking to, you know, uh..."

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:33.120
"Take this shit off of my shoulders, you know?"

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:35.840
"So, I found this discourse and sort of ran with that

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:37.460
but that didn’t really work out

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:39.020
so he tried to apologize

00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:40.510
but that didn’t cut it either.

00:02:40.510 --> 00:02:44.990
At this point, Shia turned to an obscure art
movement called metamodernism for help.

00:02:44.990 --> 00:02:46.180
Postmodernism?

00:02:46.189 --> 00:02:47.089
Metamodernism?

00:02:47.089 --> 00:02:48.180
(What the hell is the difference?)

00:02:48.189 --> 00:02:49.189
Let me explain.

00:02:49.189 --> 00:02:50.829
An easy way to grasp the difference between

00:02:50.829 --> 00:02:52.749
postmodernism and metamodernism

00:02:52.749 --> 00:02:54.400
is to think about Hollywood movies.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:57.660
An example of a postmodern movie would be
Ryan Reynolds' Deadpool

00:02:57.660 --> 00:02:59.320
a film we've talked about before.

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:00.760
Deadpool: "I'm touching myself tonight."

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:04.140
A traditional superhero like Spiderman 
believes in big ideas like

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:07.740
justice, responsibility, and truth
with an absolute sincerity.

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:10.660
Uncle Peter: "With great power, 
comes great responsibility"

00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:12.480
Deadpool says fuck all that.

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:13.769
Colossus: "Be a hero."

00:03:13.769 --> 00:03:14.749
"The way we..."

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:16.480
*vomits*

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:18.560
There are no big ideas worth
believing in.

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:19.580
For the merc with a mouth

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:22.480
the idea of truth and sincerity 
is just a movie cliche

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:25.140
to be lampooned as he massacres "bad guys"

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:26.760
for strictly personal reasons.

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:29.020
Deadpool: "Okay guys. Let's get out there and make a difference."

00:03:29.020 --> 00:03:30.540
What about metamodernism?

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:31.820
What the hell does THAT mean?

00:03:31.829 --> 00:03:35.069
If traditional Hollywood heroes 
embody sincere values

00:03:35.069 --> 00:03:37.840
while postmodern heroes wholly reject such ideas

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:40.860
then metamodernist ones fall in between

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:42.940
a kind of sincerity that is communicated

00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:45.240
through an insincere distancing.

00:03:45.249 --> 00:03:45.929
Confusing?

00:03:45.929 --> 00:03:47.929
Yeah, this stuff can get a bit nebulous.

00:03:47.929 --> 00:03:52.369
The best example of a metamodernist movie
would be Harmony Korine’s batshit crazy

00:03:52.369 --> 00:03:53.529
Spring Breakers.

00:03:53.529 --> 00:03:57.449
Our heros are four bikini clad girls on a
hedonistic rampage.

00:03:57.449 --> 00:03:58.960
We follow them as they plunge into

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:00.820
sex, drugs, and debauchery.

00:04:00.829 --> 00:04:04.109
And yet, despite over-the-top depravity of
the party scenes

00:04:04.109 --> 00:04:07.520
the girls describe their experiences
 as a sincere process of

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:09.620
self improvement
and self discovery.

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:11.340
"We saw some beautiful things here."

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:13.000
"I mean, everyone was so sweet here."

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:14.600
"So warm and friendly."

00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:18.260
Juxtaposing the quasi-spiritual tone 
of the girl’s monologue

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:19.780
with images of indulgence

00:04:19.780 --> 00:04:20.860
or mass murder

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:22.040
may seem ironic

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:24.520
"You met people who are just...like us."

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:26.940
"Who's way more than just having a good time."

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:29.720
but the girl’s epiphanies are 100% authentic.

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:31.620
In true metamodernist fashion

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:35.720
Spring Breakers uses an ironic, insincere approach to communicate something

00:04:35.720 --> 00:04:37.200
absolutely serious.

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:39.520
After spending months defending himself online

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:41.140
Labeouf came to the realization that

00:04:41.140 --> 00:04:43.680
neither clever jokes nor genuine explanation

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:44.520
were good enough.

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:48.300
He needed to do something that would show
everybody that he really was sorry.

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:49.740
He needed to be vulnerable.

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:53.100
Shia: "I had read this 'metamodern' uh manifesto."

00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:55.560
"Which...really touched me."

00:04:55.580 --> 00:04:58.620
So Labeouf created his first metamodernist artwork entitled

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:00.040
#IAMSORRY

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:02.240
a performance art installation in LA

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:04.900
where he invited the public to join him in his sorrow.

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:07.640
Audience members were to enter 
the mostly empty gallery

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:08.640
pick out a prop like

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:09.620
an Indiana Jones whip

00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:10.940
a bottle of Jack Daniels

00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:11.780
a Transformer

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:13.100
and then sit across from LaBeouf

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:15.840
who was wearing that 
infamous paper bag over his head.

00:05:15.849 --> 00:05:17.269
He wanted to show contrition

00:05:17.269 --> 00:05:18.349
but like Spring Breakers

00:05:18.349 --> 00:05:21.540
he delivered the apology so that the audience would hold it at a distance

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:24.480
making it not entirely sincere, but not entirely

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:25.490
insincere.

00:05:25.490 --> 00:05:27.830
It's all the distance and irony of postmodernism

00:05:27.830 --> 00:05:30.490
with all the sincerity of a classic narrative.

00:05:30.490 --> 00:05:33.530
Most didn’t understand what they were seeing 
or what it meant.

00:05:33.530 --> 00:05:34.910
Attendee: "Definitely pretty weird."

00:05:34.910 --> 00:05:39.580
But encountering an apologizing, 
sometimes crying, Shia LaBeouf in an LA art gallery

00:05:39.580 --> 00:05:41.080
encountering a scene that was both

00:05:41.080 --> 00:05:43.300
sincere and ironic at the same time

00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:46.080
gave them a decidedly metamodernist experience.

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:49.600
Rocco: "He was emotionally 'pouring-out' 
the whole time."

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:50.980
And even if they didn’t understand it

00:05:50.980 --> 00:05:51.800
they felt it.

00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:54.560
Attendee: "Everyone thinks he's so crazy,...but..."

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:55.640
"........"

00:05:55.640 --> 00:05:58.720
"It seemed like the least crazy thing I've seen him do 
in a while."

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:03.259
The spectacle of Shia didn’t end with an
apology, instead, the hashtags and stunts

00:06:03.259 --> 00:06:04.179
kept coming.

00:06:04.180 --> 00:06:08.320
And in each case the performances were both
ironic and sincere at the same time.

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:10.320
His “meditation for narcissists”

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:13.540
an hour long session wherein 
LaBeouf jumped rope in front of a mirror

00:06:13.540 --> 00:06:16.360
was both self-mocking and
truly meditative.

00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:17.340
His work entitled

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:18.400
#INTERVIEW

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:20.140
was both a simple minded stunt

00:06:20.140 --> 00:06:23.360
and an hour long exploration of uncomfortable intimacy.

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:23.960
His work

00:06:23.960 --> 00:06:25.300
#TOUCHMYSOUL

00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:27.300
was both ironic and sincere.

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:29.300
Shia: "Hi, you've reached LaBeouf, Rönkkö &amp; Turner."

00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:30.500
"Can you touch my soul?"

00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:32.200
And perhaps his most memeable work

00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:33.480
#INTRODUCTIONS

00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:35.740
was meant to be a shallow viral sensation

00:06:35.740 --> 00:06:38.480
while simultaneously communicating true feelings.

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:40.000
Shia: "NOTHING IS IMPOSSIBLE!"

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:44.620
#INTRODUCTIONS, wasn’t originally meant
to be an absurd version of a motivational speech

00:06:44.620 --> 00:06:45.300
Shia: "DO IT!"

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:49.920
but rather was a collaborative film project
created with the help of 36 film students.

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:54.280
Shia and his collaborators asked the students
to submit short monologues for LaBeouf to read

00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:55.940
while standing in front of a green screen.

00:06:55.940 --> 00:06:58.000
Each monologue had to evoke a feeling

00:06:58.000 --> 00:06:58.740
…any feeling.

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:01.520
The students’ work was naïve even sentimental

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:03.900
yet LaBeouf’s performance in front of the green screen

00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:05.320
was coldly calculated.

00:07:05.320 --> 00:07:07.580
Shia: "Everytime I see a man on a motorbike..."

00:07:08.200 --> 00:07:09.260
"...I think it's my dad."

00:07:09.260 --> 00:07:11.920
The work was both a viral PR stunt and

00:07:11.920 --> 00:07:13.120
entirely heartfelt.

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:15.000
And that's what metamodernism is

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:15.840
It's a mix of

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:18.260
shallow self-promotion and intimacy.

00:07:18.260 --> 00:07:20.640
It’s a process where the bland and the commercial

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:23.020
is made uncomfortable and even dangerous.

00:07:23.020 --> 00:07:25.920
A metamodernist moment is when 
what is designed to numb you

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:26.720
turns around

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:27.240
breaks

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:28.900
makes you feel something.

00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:30.560
In a world where we’re all cynical

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:33.040
we like to think that we can hold ideas like

00:07:33.040 --> 00:07:36.020
Uncle Ben’s famous line about responsibility, 
at a distance.

00:07:36.020 --> 00:07:38.300
What metamodernism does is agree with us.

00:07:38.300 --> 00:07:40.060
It encourages us to be cynical

00:07:40.060 --> 00:07:43.520
but then surprises us by making us feel the guilt we would have felt

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:45.760
if we’d taken Uncle Ben seriously.

00:07:45.760 --> 00:07:49.320
LaBeouf’s performances aim to make you take
him seriously.

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:52.320
He wants to take your cynicism about 
his fame and persona

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:53.680
and turn it against you.

00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:56.120
Shia is like the girls from Spring Breakers.

00:07:56.120 --> 00:07:59.820
They too come off as trivial people who don’t
have to be taken seriously

00:07:59.820 --> 00:08:03.320
but they turn out to be very dangerous 
and impossible to ignore.

00:08:03.480 --> 00:08:04.200
So what do you think?

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:07.800
Is Shia LaBeouf a bold new voice taking performance
art to the next level?

00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:10.880
Or is he just a bored celebrity 
pulling pranks on us all?

00:08:10.880 --> 00:08:12.720
Let us know what you think in 
the comments.

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:14.640
Shia: "I know I can't single-handedly save the world"

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:15.980
"but I'd like to, like, try."

